WN.lb(4,{"mt_ukLvUD8DFA":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is an inference?","choices":["Working something out from clues in the text","A wild guess with no reason","Copying a sentence from the text"],"answer":0,"hint":"How did you know Mia was angry?","explain":"An inference uses clues, like slammed and threw, to work out what is not written."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"Nina's eyes filled with tears as the bus drove away with her best friend on it.\" How does Nina feel?","choices":["Sad","Bored","Proud"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do tears and a friend leaving tell you?","explain":"Tears as her best friend leaves are the clue that Nina is sad."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You say Max is scared. Which is the best way to back it up?","choices":["Quote: \"Max froze and gripped the rail.\"","Say: \"I just think he is scared.\"","Say: \"Max is nine years old.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence points to the text, not just your opinion.","explain":"Quoting froze and gripped the rail proves the inference. An opinion or an unrelated fact does not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"First you think Oscar is rude for not saying hi. Then you read he had headphones on. What now?","choices":["Change the idea: he did not hear","Keep thinking he is rude","Ignore the headphones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does your first idea still fit every clue?","explain":"The headphones clue does not fit rude, so a good reader changes the inference."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"\"'Get out!' she shouted.\" Which feeling fits best?","choices":["Angry","Calm","Sleepy"],"answer":0,"hint":"How does the speech verb say the words are spoken?","explain":"Get out spoken with shouted shows anger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"\"Jay gave his lunch to the new boy, who had none.\" What is Jay's motive?","choices":["He wants to be kind and help","He hates his lunch","He wants to get in trouble"],"answer":0,"hint":"A motive is the reason. Why give food to someone with none?","explain":"Giving lunch to a boy who had none shows a kind motive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"Priya practised her lines {n} times and couldn't eat breakfast.\" What can you infer?","choices":["She is nervous about a performance","She does not like breakfast","She has forgotten her lines"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why practise {n} times and skip breakfast?","explain":"Practising {n} times and not eating are clues that she is nervous about performing.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"\"Dan grinned when he saw {n} presents with his name on.\" Which part is inferred?","choices":["Dan is happy","Dan saw the presents","There were {n} presents"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is not written but worked out from grinned?","explain":"The text says he grinned and saw {n} presents. That he is happy is worked out from the grin.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"Kim kicked a stone and stared at the ground.\" How might Kim feel?","choices":["Upset or fed up","Excited","We can not tell at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do kicking a stone and staring at the ground show?","explain":"Kicking a stone and staring down are clues to being upset. The text shows it without saying it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"\"Zara checked the clock {n} times before the bell.\" What can you infer?","choices":["She is eager for the bell to ring","She has a new clock","She does not care about time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why would someone keep checking the time?","explain":"Checking the clock {n} times is the clue: she is waiting eagerly, maybe impatient, for the bell.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Ali's face went red. He hid the broken vase behind his back.\" Which is stated, not inferred?","choices":["Ali hid the vase","Ali feels guilty","Ali broke the vase"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is written in the words?","explain":"He hid the vase is in the text. Guilty and broke it are worked out from the red face and hiding."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ruby waited {n} minutes in the rain for her friend, who never came. She sighed and walked home. Best evidence she is disappointed?","choices":["She sighed and walked home","It was raining","She waited {n} minutes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence of a feeling is something Ruby does that shows it.","explain":"Sighing and walking home shows how Ruby feels. Rain and the {n} minutes set the scene but do not show her feeling on their own.","params":{"n":[10,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A story might never say Mia is angry. It might say she slammed the door and threw her bag. Those actions are clues. When you use clues to work something out, you are making an inference.","Some things the text says. His hands shook is right there in the words. Other things you work out. Sam is nervous is not written, but the shaking hands show it.","An inference needs proof. Point to what the character does or says. Leo is Ben's brother is true, but it does not show jealousy. He muttered, mine is better anyway, does.","A motive is the reason someone acts. Read on and test your idea. If a new clue does not fit, change your idea. Words like snapped, gasped and sighed show feelings too."],"adult":["Inference means working out what the text does not state, using clues it does give. For characters, the clues are actions, dialogue, how lines are spoken, and how others react. Readers aged 7 to 10 are expected to infer feelings, thoughts and motives and to justify each inference with evidence from the text.","The lab separates the skills. Learners tap action words that reveal a feeling, sort statements into what the text says and what we work out, build a because with two real pieces of evidence, revise a motive one clue at a time, and match speech verbs such as snapped and sighed to feelings.","Common mistakes: refusing to infer because it is not written; treating a wild guess as an inference; citing a true but irrelevant fact as evidence; restating the claim (I think she is nervous) instead of quoting; fixing on a first idea and ignoring later clues; and mixing up what is stated with what is inferred.","When reading together, ask how a character feels, then ask what in the text makes you think so. Expect a quote or an action, not just an opinion."]},"familyTip":"Pause a story or a TV scene and ask, \"How is that character feeling? What did they do or say that tells you?\" Then ask, \"Did anything later change your mind?\"","keyIdea":"Writers often show a feeling instead of naming it. You work it out from what a character does and says, and how they say it. That is an inference. A good inference fits every clue, and you can point to the exact words that prove it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"\"Ella's voice wobbled. She held her notes tight.\" Is each card in the text, or worked out? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["The text says it","We work it out"],"items":[["Her voice wobbled",0],["She held her notes tight",0],["Ella is nervous",1],["Ella is about to speak to a group",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaginfer","caption":"Flagship lab: tap the actions that give a feeling away, sort said from worked out, prove it with evidence, revise a motive clue by clue, read how lines are said","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_mLPEMpYb_R":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Story: The cat sat on the roof and could not get down. Where was the cat?","choices":["On the roof","In a basket","Up my tree"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for the place word in the story.","explain":"The story says the cat sat on the roof. A basket or your tree are not in the story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Story: Leo and his mum went to the zoo. They saw {n} lions asleep in the sun. How many lions did they see?","choices":["{n}","{=n+2}","Lots"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for the number the story gave, not a guess.","explain":"The story said {n} lions, so the answer is {n}. Lots is too fuzzy, and {=n+2} was never said.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The reader says a word you have never heard. What should you do?","choices":["Ask what the word means","Pretend you know it","Stop listening"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can you find out what a new word means?","explain":"Asking what a word means helps the rest of the story make sense. Pretending leaves you lost."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Story: Pip's boat had a hole, so it began to sink. What went wrong?","choices":["The boat had a hole","Pip had a boat","Boats are fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"A problem is something that goes wrong. What was bad?","explain":"The hole is the problem, because it made the boat sink. Having a boat is just a detail."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Story: Sita baked {n} buns, and then she ate one. How many buns did she bake?","choices":["{n}","{=n-1}","One"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question asks how many she baked, before she ate any.","explain":"She baked {n} buns. {=n-1} is how many were left after she ate one, which is a different question.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Fact: Bees make honey in a hive. Your friend says bees live in shoes. Where do bees make honey?","choices":["In a hive","In shoes","In a shop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use what the fact said, not what your friend said.","explain":"The fact says bees make honey in a hive. Your friend's idea is not in the text."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Story: First the egg cracked. Next a chick came out. Last it said cheep. What happened first?","choices":["The egg cracked","It said cheep","A chick came out"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part did the story say first?","explain":"The egg cracked first. The chick came out next, and it said cheep last."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Story: Nell wore her boots because it was snowing. Why did Nell wear boots?","choices":["It was snowing","Her boots are pink","She likes dogs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for the word because. What comes after it?","explain":"The story says because it was snowing. The word because tells you the reason."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Story: Tom's dog ran away in the rain. Tom looked everywhere and found it under a bench. What went wrong?","choices":["The dog ran away","It was a dog","Tom found it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What went wrong means the problem. What was bad in the story?","explain":"The problem is that the dog ran away. Finding it under the bench is how the problem got fixed."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Story: Zara picked {n} red apples. Question: How many apples did Zara pick?","choices":["{n}","{=n+1}","I like apples"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for the number in the story, not a number you like.","explain":"The story said Zara picked {n} apples, so the answer is {n}. The answer comes from the words you heard.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Story: First Raj got wet in the rain. Next he dried off. Last he had hot soup. What happened next?","choices":["He dried off","He had hot soup","He got wet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the part that came after getting wet and before the soup.","explain":"Raj got wet first, then dried off, then had soup. Dried off is the middle part, so it came next."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Story: Kim had {n} toy cars. She gave one to her friend. Your friend has lots of cars. How many cars did Kim have at the start?","choices":["{n}","Lots","One"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question asks about Kim in the story, not about your friend.","explain":"Kim had {n} cars at the start. Your friend's cars are not in the story, and one is the car she gave away.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When a grown-up reads to you, you do not have to read. You just listen. Your ears do the work.","Listen for three things. Who is in it? Where are they? What goes wrong? Mia's kite got stuck in a tree. That is what went wrong.","Answers come from the story. The kite in the story was red. Your kite may be blue, but the answer is still red.","Can you tell it back? Say what came first, next and last. Is a word new? Ask what it means. Then the rest makes sense."],"adult":["This topic is about understanding books that are read to a child, often books harder than the child could read alone. Listening comprehension runs ahead of reading for years, so read-alouds are where children meet richer stories, facts and words. Children show they understood by answering and asking questions about key details, retelling events, and asking when something is unclear.","The lab reads a short story one line at a time, with a picture for each line and an optional spoken voice. The child then answers who, where and what went wrong, taps the exact line that holds a detail, puts three events in order, and finds a new word in a fact text about owls, asks what it means, and uses the answer.","Common mistakes: answering from their own life instead of the text, such as saying blue because their own kite is blue; naming a true detail, like the colour of the kite, when asked for the problem; inventing a character who was never there; retelling events out of order; and nodding along past a word they did not know.","When you read together, pause once or twice to ask who, where or what went wrong. If your child answers from their own life, ask, what did the book say? Point to the page together. Invite them to stop you when a word is new, and praise them when they do."]},"familyTip":"After a bedtime story, ask your child to tell it back using first, next and last. Then ask one question whose answer is in the book, such as where did they go, and find the page together to check.","keyIdea":"When someone reads to you, listen for who is in it, where it happens and what goes wrong. Your answers come from the words you heard, not from your own life. You can tell the story back in order. If a word or part is not clear, ask the reader.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the answer come from the story? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["From the story","Not from the story"],"items":[["Mia had a red kite",0],["Grandpa got a ladder",0],["The kite got stuck",0],["My kite is blue",1],["A pirate came",1],["I went to the beach",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagaloud","caption":"Flagship lab: hear a story line by line, answer who, where and what went wrong, find answers in the text, retell it in order and ask about a new word.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_dmFnJzxKwz":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Claim: Sam is excited about the trip. Which evidence is strongest?","choices":["“Sam bounced on her toes, grinning at the coach.”","“The coach was late.”","“Sam wore a green coat.”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which quotation shows Sam herself, and fits excitement?","explain":"Bouncing and grinning show Sam herself and suggest excitement. The late coach is setting, and the coat is irrelevant."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which explanation analyses the quotation “he crept down the stairs”?","choices":["“Crept” suggests he does not want to be heard.","This shows that he crept down the stairs.","This shows that he is sneaky."],"answer":0,"hint":"One retells, one repeats a claim, one zooms in on a word. Which zooms in?","explain":"Analysis names the word and what it suggests. The others retell the quotation or repeat the claim."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The text says “Kai’s hands were shaking.” Is “Kai is afraid” stated or inferred?","choices":["Inferred: the shaking is a clue","Stated: the text says so","Neither: we can not use it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the text actually use the word afraid?","explain":"The text states the shaking hands. Fear is an inference from that clue, and it is a fair one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is “Zara read every book in the library twice” strong evidence that she loves reading?","choices":["It shows a habit kept up over time","Because it is a long quotation","Because it mentions books"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does every book, twice, tell you about how often?","explain":"It shows a repeated choice over a long time, which is what loving reading looks like. Length and topic words do not make it strong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ellie “gave away {n} of her own books”. Which sentence embeds this correctly?","choices":["Ellie “gave away {n} of her own books”, which shows her generosity.","Ellie is generous. “She gave away {n} of her own books.”","Ellie is generous “books”."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one reads as a single smooth sentence?","explain":"The first fits the quotation into its own sentence. The second is dropped in, and the third breaks the grammar.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Claim: Dev is hungry. Which quotation is best trimmed?","choices":["“his stomach growled”","the whole page about the kitchen","“a”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one keeps the proof and drops the rest?","explain":"His stomach growled is short and proves the point. A whole page is too long, and a proves nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mr Hale “sighed {n} times” in the meeting. Which explanation analyses this?","choices":["Repeated sighing suggests his patience is running out.","This shows he sighed {n} times.","This shows he is impatient."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one says what the action suggests, not just what happened?","explain":"Saying that repeated sighing suggests patience running out is analysis. The others retell or restate.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Claim: Nia is brave. Which evidence does NOT support it?","choices":["“Nia had red hair.”","“Nia stepped forward when no one else would.”","“Her voice shook, but she kept speaking.”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line has nothing to do with bravery?","explain":"Red hair is true but irrelevant. Stepping forward and speaking through fear both show courage."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Claim: Priya is exhausted. Which evidence is strongest?","choices":["“Her eyelids drooped as she slumped over the desk.”","“The classroom clock ticked loudly.”","“Priya had a blue pencil case.”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which quotation is about Priya herself and can only mean tiredness?","explain":"Drooping eyelids and slumping show tiredness in Priya herself. The clock is setting, and the pencil case is irrelevant."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ravi “checked his phone {n} times” during the film. Is this good evidence that he is bored?","choices":["Yes: a repeated action that shows it","No: it only mentions a phone","No: numbers can not be evidence"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the action happen once or again and again, and what does that suggest?","explain":"Checking a phone {n} times is a repeated action by Ravi, so it is strong evidence of boredom or distraction.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence embeds the quotation correctly?","choices":["The sea “roared against the rocks”, which makes it sound dangerous.","The sea is dangerous. “The sea roared against the rocks all night.”","The sea is dangerous “roared”."],"answer":0,"hint":"Read each one aloud. Which still sounds like one proper sentence?","explain":"The first one fits the quotation into its own grammar. The second drops a whole line in, and the third breaks the sentence."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your quotation is {n} words long, but only 4 of them prove your point. What should you do?","choices":["Cut it down to the words that prove it","Keep all {n} words","Add more words around it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a longer quotation stronger, or just harder to analyse?","explain":"Cut the {n} words down to the ones that do the work. Length does not make evidence stronger.","params":{"n":[10,16]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When you make a point about a text, you need proof. The proof is evidence: the exact words the writer used. Quote them, and the reader can check your idea for themselves.","Not all evidence is equal. Say your claim is that Maya is nervous. The hall was packed is weak, because it is about the room, not Maya. She gripped her script so hard that the pages crumpled is strong. It shows Maya herself, and it can only mean tension.","Keep quotations short. Pick only the words that do the work, then fit them into your own sentence: Maya gripped her script so hard that the pages crumpled. Do not just drop a whole line in after a full stop.","Then explain. Do not retell the quote, and do not just repeat your claim. Zoom in on a word. The verb gripped suggests force she cannot control, so her body gives her nerves away."],"adult":["This topic asks learners to back up points with exact words from a text. That covers what the text says and what we can work out from it. They also judge which quote best supports a point, and they explain the link. For ages 11 to 14, the goal moves from giving any quote to giving the one that fits best.","The lab splits the skill into parts. Learners match each claim to the line that proves it. They rate quotes as strong, weak or off the point. They cut a sentence down to the shortest quote that still works. They fit quotes into their own sentences. Last, they pick the explanation that looks closely at a word.","Common mistakes: picking a quote about the setting or a different person, not the one in the claim; picking a quote that is true but beside the point; thinking a long quote is a strong one; dropping a quote in as its own sentence; retelling the quote or repeating the claim instead of explaining it; and treating a guess as if the text said it.","Ask your learner why one quote is better than another. Good answers say whether it is about the right person, whether it could mean something else, and which single word carries the meaning."]},"familyTip":"When your child says something about a book, film or song, ask \"Which exact words make you think that?\" and then \"Is there a line that proves it even better?\"","keyIdea":"Back up every point about a text with exact words from it. Strong evidence is about the thing you are claiming and fits only one reading. Keep quotations short, build them into your own sentence, then explain which words prove your point and how.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Claim: Ben is jealous of his brother. How well does each quotation support it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Strong","Weak","Does not support"],"items":[["“Ben glared at his brother’s new bike.”",0],["“Mine is better anyway,” Ben muttered.",0],["“Ben went quiet at dinner.”",1],["“Everyone admired the new bike.”",1],["“The bike was bright red.”",2],["“Ben was twelve.”",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagevid","caption":"Flagship lab: find the line that proves a claim, rate quotations, trim a quotation, embed it in your own sentence and explain it.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NJu_l5_Xhp":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which lens asks how empire and colonised people are shown?","choices":["Postcolonial","Marxist","Ecocritical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which lens looks at empire, colonies and who tells the story?","explain":"Postcolonial critics ask how empire and colonised people are shown, as Edward Said did with Mansfield Park."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A critic says Elizabeth Bennet's family may lose their home because only a male heir can inherit it. Which lens is that?","choices":["Feminist","Ecocritical","Postcolonial"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this point about gender, nature or empire?","explain":"Inheritance passing only to men is a point about gender, so it is a feminist reading."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Your reading of a novel is bold, but you cannot find any words in it that back it up. What should you do?","choices":["Change or drop it: a reading needs textual evidence","Keep it: lenses let you say anything","Say the author secretly meant it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes a reading strong rather than a guess?","explain":"A lens opens new questions, but the answers have to come from the words. With no evidence it is only a guess."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"“Mind-forg’d manacles” in London are chains the mind makes for itself. Which lens fits this reading best?","choices":["Psychoanalytic","Marxist","Ecocritical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which lens looks for hidden fears and the inner mind?","explain":"Chains made by the mind point to fears people have taken inside, which is the psychoanalytic question."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A reading quotes {n} phrases from the poem, and each is really there. A classmate objects that Blake did not intend it. Is that a strong objection?","choices":["No: a lens reading stands on textual evidence, not intent","Yes: only intent counts","Yes, unless there are more than {n} phrases"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a lens reading a claim about the author's plan?","explain":"With {n} real phrases as evidence, the reading stands. Intent is not the test: the words are.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"An essay spends {n} paragraphs explaining feminist theory, but quotes the novel only once. What is its main weakness?","choices":["Too little close reading of the text","Too few lenses","It should explain theory for even longer"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do examiners reward: theory or close reading?","explain":"{n} paragraphs of theory with one quotation means too little close reading. Use the lens to read the words.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What lifts a critical essay into the top band?","choices":["Weighing your reading against another one","Using every lens in one paragraph","Retelling the plot in detail"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the last move in a strong paragraph.","explain":"Top essays develop one reading, then weigh it against an alternative. Piling up lenses or retelling the plot does not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a strong critical paragraph, what comes straight after your claim?","choices":["A quotation that backs it","An alternative reading","A summary of the plot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Claim, then what proves it, then analysis.","explain":"After the claim comes the quotation that backs it, then analysis of the words, then an alternative reading."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"{n} students read London through {n} different lenses, and each backs their reading with quotations. How many readings can be valid?","choices":["All {n}, if each is backed by the text","Only one of them","None, because only Blake knows"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a poem support more than one strong reading at the same time?","explain":"All {n} can be valid. A text can hold many strong readings, as long as each one is backed by words on the page.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Your essay has {n} quotations but never makes a claim through a lens. What is missing?","choices":["A clear claim about what the lens reveals","Even more quotations","A longer plot summary"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quotations are evidence. Evidence for what?","explain":"{n} quotations prove nothing without a claim. Start with what the lens shows you, then use the quotations to back it.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which question does a Marxist lens ask?","choices":["Who has money and power, and who does the work?","How is nature shown?","What hidden fears drive people?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Marxist critics focus on class, money and work.","explain":"A Marxist lens asks about money, power, class and work. Nature is the ecocritical question, and hidden fears the psychoanalytic one."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A critic says Blake's “charter’d Thames” shows a river turned into property. Which lens is that?","choices":["Ecocritical","Feminist","Psychoanalytic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which lens asks how people treat nature?","explain":"Ecocritics ask how nature is shown and used. A river being owned is a classic ecocritical point."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A classmate says your feminist reading of London is wrong because Blake was not a feminist. Best reply?","choices":["A lens reading is judged on the text, not on the author's views","You are right, so I will drop it","Every reading is right anyway"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a lens a claim about what the author planned?","explain":"A lens is a question you bring to the text. It is judged on the words that back it, not on what Blake believed."}],"explain":{"kid":["A critical lens is a set of questions you bring to a text. Each lens makes you notice different things. A Marxist lens asks who has money and power, and who does the work. A feminist lens asks how women and gender roles are shown. A postcolonial lens asks how empire and colonised people are shown.","An ecocritical lens asks how nature is shown and how people treat it. A psychoanalytic lens looks for hidden fears and wishes. Take Blake's poem London from 1794. A Marxist sees the soldier bleeding on the palace walls. An ecocritic sees that even the river is owned.","One text can hold many strong readings. But not anything goes. A good reading points to the words that back it. If no words back it, it is a guess.","A lens is not a claim about what the writer planned. Blake was not reading Marx. You are asking new questions of old words. The best essays weigh one reading against another."],"adult":["This topic introduces the main critical lenses used at A level and beyond: Marxist, feminist, postcolonial, ecocritical and psychoanalytic. Each is best taught as a set of questions the reader brings, which lets students produce several readings of one text.","The lab uses public-domain texts. Learners match each lens to its question, hunt for the phrase in Blake's London that backs each reading, sort readings of the first line of Pride and Prejudice by whether the words support them, name the lens a critic is using, and build a paragraph in order.","A common mistake is to think a lens reading claims the author's intention, so that a feminist reading of Blake is wrong because he was not a feminist. Another is the belief that there is one right reading, or the opposite, that any reading goes. In fact readings are judged on textual evidence.","Exam boards reward students who consider different interpretations. At home, pick a film you have both seen and ask your teen who has the power in it, and what the story says about women. Then ask which scene proves it."]},"familyTip":"Next time you watch a film together, ask one lens question, such as who has the money and power here, and then ask your teen which scene backs their answer.","keyIdea":"A critical lens is a set of questions you bring to a text: Marxist critics ask about money, power and work; feminist critics about gender; postcolonial critics about empire; ecocritics about nature; psychoanalytic critics about hidden fears and wishes. One text can support many strong readings, but a reading is only as good as the words that back it, and it is not a claim about what the author intended. Top essays weigh one reading against another.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each reading of Blake's London backed by the words? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Backed by the words","Not backed"],"items":[["The “Palace walls” show the rulers stained by the soldier's death",0],["The “charter’d Thames” shows nature owned for profit",0],["The “youthful Harlots curse” shows women harmed by poverty",0],["London is a happy poem about a holiday",1],["Blake secretly loved the king",1],["The poem is set in Paris",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglens","caption":"Flagship lab: match lenses to their questions, find evidence in Blake's London, test readings of Austen and build a critical paragraph.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Hqz5y_tWz2":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is the best description of a climax?","choices":["The turning point, where tension is highest","The last page of the story","The most exciting description"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the top of the story mountain.","explain":"The climax is the turning point at peak tension. It usually comes near the end, but the resolution follows it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Maya rows alone and is beaten back by the waves. Next she asks the crew for help. Why does she ask?","choices":["Rowing alone has failed","It is spring","She always liked the crew"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the event just before her choice.","explain":"Her failure alone forces the choice. One event causes the next. That is what makes her change believable."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A student writes: 'Tom changes a lot.' What would make this a strong point?","choices":["Naming the event that changes him and quoting evidence","Saying it again in longer words","Describing the setting"],"answer":0,"hint":"A claim about change needs a cause and proof.","explain":"A strong point names the event that pushes Tom to change and quotes what he does before and after."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In a mystery, every clue makes the detective more worried and the danger closer. Which stage is this?","choices":["Rising action","Exposition","Falling action"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the tension going up or down?","explain":"The tension keeps climbing, so this is rising action. Falling action comes after the climax, when tension drops."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Maya's tension line reads 1, 3, 5, {n}, 4, 2. Which event is the turning point?","choices":["Event 4","Event 6","Event 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the highest point on the line.","explain":"Event 4 has tension {n}, the highest score, so it is the turning point. The events after it fall back to 4 and 2.","params":{"n":[7,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A story has {n} chapters. The climax is in chapter {=n-1}. Which chapter most likely holds the resolution?","choices":["Chapter {n}","Chapter 1","Chapter {=n-2}"],"answer":0,"hint":"The resolution comes after the climax, at the end.","explain":"The resolution is the new normal after the climax, so it is in chapter {n}, the last one. Chapter {=n-2} comes before the climax.","params":{"n":[6,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A villain is cruel on page 1 and cruel on the last page, with no change. What can you say?","choices":["The villain has no arc","The villain is the setting","The story has no plot"],"answer":0,"hint":"An arc is the change a character goes through. Compare the start and the end.","explain":"With no change, the villain has no arc. That can be a choice. Often only the main character changes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A story about a lost child is set in a busy market on a festival day. How might the setting shape the plot?","choices":["The crowds make the child harder to find","It has no effect at all","It tells us the ending"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the place make easier or harder?","explain":"The crowd makes the search harder and raises the tension. The setting drives events instead of sitting in the background."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a story, a boy finds a map in his grandad's attic. This starts the whole adventure. Which stage is this?","choices":["Inciting incident","Climax","Resolution"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which stage starts the problem or the quest?","explain":"Finding the map sets the story moving, so it is the inciting incident. The climax comes much later, at peak tension."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A novel's climax is in chapter {n}. The resolution is in the last chapter. Where is the falling action?","choices":["After chapter {n}, before the last chapter","Before chapter {n}","In chapter 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Falling action is what happens as a result of the climax.","explain":"Falling action follows the climax, so it starts in chapter {=n+1} and runs until the resolution. Before chapter {n} is rising action.","params":{"n":[8,14]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ana is shy at the start. At the end she gives a speech to the whole town. What has the writer shown?","choices":["A character arc","A setting","An exposition"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do we call the way a character changes over a story?","explain":"Ana changes from shy to confident, so the writer has shown a character arc. To prove it, find the event that made her change."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A reader scores the tension of five events: 2, 4, {n}, 9, 3. Which event is the climax?","choices":["Event 4","Event 3","Event 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"The climax is where tension peaks.","explain":"Event 4 scores 9, the highest, so it is the climax. Event 3 scores {n}, which is less than 9, so it is still rising action.","params":{"n":[5,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A story is set on a ship in a fog. The fog hides the rocks, and the ship hits them. What role does the setting play?","choices":["It causes the problem","It is only decoration","It is the resolution"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would the crash happen on a clear day?","explain":"The fog hides the rocks, so the setting causes the crash. Without the fog, this plot falls apart."}],"explain":{"kid":["Most stories have a shape like a mountain. The start sets up who, where and when. Then something goes wrong. That is the inciting incident. Things get harder, and the tension climbs.","The top of the mountain is the climax. It is the turning point, where the tension is highest. It is not the last page. After it, the results play out, and the story settles into a new normal.","Events do not just happen one after another. Each one pushes the next. Ask why a character makes a choice. The answer is often the event just before it.","Good characters change. Maya starts out saying she needs no one. By the end she leads a crew. That change is her arc. Look for the moment she turns, and the proof in what she does. The setting matters too. Take away the storm and her whole story falls apart."],"adult":["This topic asks learners to explain how a plot unfolds through key events and how characters respond and change. They should also link character, setting and plot. At ages 11 to 14 the aim is analysis. Learners should not just retell the story. They should say why each event matters and point to evidence.","The lab uses one short original story. Learners deal event cards onto a story mountain. They shape a tension line so the climax is the peak. They plot the main character's arc from quoted evidence, and link each key event to its cause. Last, they swap the setting and see which events can no longer happen.","Common mistakes: putting the climax on the last page; calling every exciting moment the climax; retelling events as a list with no cause; saying a character changed with no event or evidence to show it; and treating the setting as decoration when it drives the problem.","When your child reads or watches a story, ask three questions. Where is the turning point? How is the main character different at the end, and what made them change? Would the story still work in a different place or time?"]},"familyTip":"After a film or a book, sketch a quick story mountain together on paper. Mark the turning point. Then ask: how did the main character change, and which event made it happen?","keyIdea":"A plot is a chain of events with a shape. Tension rises to a turning point, the climax, then falls to a new normal. Along the way the main character is pushed to change, and that change is the character arc. The setting is not just a backdrop. It can cause the problem and force the choices.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Before or after the climax? Drag each event, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Before the climax","After the climax"],"items":[["The storm warning comes",0],["Maya is beaten back by waves",0],["Maya rows alone and proud",0],["They tow the boat home",1],["Maya joins the crew",1],["The town cheers them in",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplot","caption":"Flagship lab: build a story mountain, peak the tension, plot a character arc, link causes, swap the setting.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_-n7sDQHHYF":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In 1606, who did most people believe had chosen the king?","choices":["God","The people, by voting","The richest nobles"],"answer":0,"hint":"This belief is called the divine right of kings.","explain":"Under the divine right of kings, God chose the monarch. King James wrote that kings answer to God alone, so a murdered king was an attack on God's order."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The witches chant, “Fair is foul, and foul is fair.” What does it mean?","choices":["Things that look good may be evil, and evil may look good","The weather is bad today","Fair people are always foul"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair can mean good or lovely. Foul means evil or ugly.","explain":"The witches warn that nothing is what it seems. That idea runs through the play: Macbeth looks loyal while planning murder."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In Act 4, the witches show Macbeth a line of eight kings who come from Banquo. Why might this please the first audience?","choices":["King James was said to descend from Banquo","Banquo was Shakespeare's father","Audiences liked long parades"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who was the king in 1606, and whose family was he said to come from?","explain":"King James was said to come from Banquo's family, so the scene flatters him. It also explains why Banquo is shown as loyal and good."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Lady Macbeth asks spirits to “unsex me here.” Which belief of the time is she going against?","choices":["Women should be gentle and obey their husbands","Women should rule the country","Women should fight in wars"],"answer":0,"hint":"She asks to lose something her time expected of women. What was it?","explain":"Women were expected to be gentle and obedient. She asks to lose that softness so she can plan a murder, which would have shocked the audience."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A student quotes {n} lines of a speech in a row and writes nothing about them. What is missing?","choices":["A paraphrase and a comment on what the words show","More lines of quotation","The date the play was written"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quotes are evidence. What must you do with evidence?","explain":"Long quotes on their own prove nothing. Quote a few words, say what they mean in your own words, then explain what they show about the character or theme.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The Porter jokes about an “equivocator” at the gate of hell. Why would that land in 1606?","choices":["A priest linked to the Gunpowder Plot had just defended equivocation at his trial","Equivocators were a type of soldier","It was a popular song"],"answer":0,"hint":"To equivocate is to twist words to avoid the truth. Who was in the news for it?","explain":"In 1606 a priest, Henry Garnet, was tried for his part in the Gunpowder Plot and defended equivocation. The joke ties Macbeth's lies to a real treason against the king."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Early on, Macbeth is called “brave Macbeth.” At the end, he is “this dead butcher.” What does this show?","choices":["He changes from a hero to a tyrant","He was always a butcher","Nothing about him changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the two names. What happened in between?","explain":"The names bookend his change: from a loyal, brave soldier to a murderous tyrant. Quoting the start and the end is how to prove a character changes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why did the witches frighten audiences in 1606 more than most people today?","choices":["Many, King James included, believed witches were real and served the devil","Witches were a new idea in 1606","The actors wore scary masks"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did King James write a book about in 1597?","explain":"King James wrote Daemonologie, a book about witches, and witch trials were real. To the first audience, the witches were a real danger, not fantasy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A messenger says, “I shall be with thee ere {n} days are past.” What does he promise?","choices":["I will be with you before {n} days have passed.","I will be with you after {n} days.","Where will you be in {n} days?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Swap thee for you and ere for before.","explain":"Thee is you and ere is before, so he will arrive within {n} days, not after them. Reading ere as after reverses the promise.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Juliet says, “Wherefore art thou Romeo?” What is she asking?","choices":["Why are you Romeo, a Montague?","Where are you, Romeo?","Who are you, Romeo?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wherefore is an old word. Is it where, why or who?","explain":"Wherefore means why. Juliet knows where Romeo is not the point: she wishes he were not from the family her own family hates."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A king says, “Thou hast but {n} days to live.” What does this mean in modern English?","choices":["You have only {n} days to live.","Where are your {n} days to live?","They have {n} days to live."],"answer":0,"hint":"Swap thou for you and hast for have. What does but mean here?","explain":"Thou is you and hast is have. But here means only, so the line is: you have only {n} days to live.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"After Duncan's murder, his horses turn wild and eat each other. Why does Shakespeare include this?","choices":["Nature itself is thrown into disorder by killing a king","To add a scene about animals","To show the horses were hungry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the Great Chain of Being. What happens when one link breaks?","explain":"A king was believed to be chosen by God. Killing him breaks the chain, so nature turns upside down: animals attack their betters and day becomes night."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Lady Macbeth says, “Ere {n} o’clock, the deed is done.” What does ere mean?","choices":["Before","After","Where"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ere is an old word for time. Try each choice in the sentence.","explain":"Ere means before. The line means: before {n} o’clock, the deed is done.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which sentence uses context best?","choices":["People in 1606 believed God chose kings, so Duncan's murder is a crime against heaven.","Shakespeare was born in 1564.","Macbeth is a play with five acts."],"answer":0,"hint":"Good context explains something in the play. Which one does?","explain":"Only the first links a belief of the time to an event in the play. A birth date or the number of acts explains nothing about what the play means."}],"explain":{"kid":["Shakespeare wrote Macbeth in about 1606. His English is early modern English. Most of the words are ours. A few small ones change the meaning, so learn them first. Thou means you. Art means are. Hath means has. Wherefore means why, not where.","To paraphrase, put a line into your own words. Keep the meaning, not the picture. When Macbeth says, “Stars, hide your fires,” he does not want the stars to die. He wants it dark, so no one sees the murder he is planning.","Now read it like his first audience. Many people then believed God ranked all things in a chain. God was at the top, then angels, then the king, then nobles, then everyone else, then animals. So killing a king was a crime against God. After Duncan dies, the play shows nature going wrong. Day stays dark, an owl kills a falcon, and horses eat each other.","The king who watched this play was James the First. He wrote a book about witches and believed they were real. He was also said to come from the family of Banquo. So the witches were truly scary to that audience, and Banquo is shown as good. Use context like this to explain a line, not as a fact stuck on the end."],"adult":["This topic asks learners to read a Shakespeare play in depth. They follow plot, character and theme through early modern English. They also link ideas to what people in the time of Elizabeth and James believed about kings, women, fate and order. The lab uses Macbeth, the most widely taught play at this stage.","In the lab, learners match old words to their meanings and fix paraphrases of a key speech. They rebuild the Great Chain of Being and find the signs of nature in disorder after the murder. Then they link details of the play to events of 1606, such as King James's book on witches and the trial of a priest tied to the Gunpowder Plot.","Common mistakes: reading wherefore as where; paraphrasing word by word, so the images are taken literally; judging the play only by modern values; and adding context that explains nothing, such as Shakespeare's birth date. Another slip is to say a character changes without quoting the start and the end.","Ask your teenager to paraphrase one famous speech aloud, line by line. Then ask how the first audience would have felt about it, and why. A strong answer links a belief of the time to particular words in the play."]},"familyTip":"Pick a famous line, such as “Fair is foul, and foul is fair.” Take turns putting it into everyday words, then ask: why would this have scared people who believed witches were real?","keyIdea":"Shakespeare's English is mostly our English, with a few old words to swap, such as thou for you and wherefore for why. Paraphrase a speech to find what the character means, not just the picture the words paint. Then read it the way a 1606 audience would: they believed God chose the king and that witches were real, so killing King Duncan breaks the whole order of nature.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this context explain the play? Drag each fact, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Explains the play","Bolted on"],"items":[["People believed God chose kings",0],["King James feared witches",0],["James was said to descend from Banquo",0],["Shakespeare was born in 1564",1],["The Globe was round",1],["Shakespeare had three children",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbard","caption":"Flagship lab: decode old words, paraphrase Macbeth's speech, rebuild the Great Chain of Being, spot nature in disorder and link the play to 1606.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_x2sWtfTeYT":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"“Tom stared at the sea. He wondered if his dad would come. Behind him, Mum quietly hoped he would not.” Which perspective?","choices":["Omniscient","Third person limited","First person"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whose thoughts do we hear: Tom's only, or Mum's too?","explain":"We hear Tom's wondering and Mum's secret hope. Entering more than one mind is the mark of an omniscient narrator."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A story uses she on every one of its {n} pages. What can you say for sure?","choices":["It is third person, limited or omniscient","It is omniscient","The narrator is a girl"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the pronoun tell you how many minds we enter?","explain":"She tells us it is third person. To decide limited or omniscient, check whose thoughts we get: one mind or many.","params":{"n":[120,400]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The reader knows the bridge is down. The hero races toward it at night. What is this called?","choices":["Dramatic irony","A plot twist","An unreliable narrator"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the reader already know, or find out later?","explain":"We already know about the danger and the hero does not. That is dramatic irony. A twist would surprise the reader later."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A narrator says, “I am perfectly calm,” then screams at a ticking clock. Why does this matter?","choices":["His actions show we cannot trust his words","It proves he really is calm","It shows the author is angry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Test what he says against what he does.","explain":"His behaviour contradicts his claim, which marks him as unreliable. Poe uses this in The Tell-Tale Heart, where the narrator insists he is sane."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A first-person narrator is cruel to everyone she meets for {n} chapters. Is the author cruel?","choices":["No, the narrator is a voice the author made up","Yes, the author is speaking","Yes, if the book won prizes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the narrator the same person as the writer?","explain":"Authors invent narrators, including nasty or foolish ones, often so readers judge them. Treat the narrator as a character, not the writer.","params":{"n":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The same fight is told once by Kai and once by Rosa. Why do the two versions differ?","choices":["Each knows and feels different things","One of them must be the author","Fights can only be told in third person"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can each character see and think?","explain":"Each teller only knows their own thoughts and what they saw, and each wants to look good. Comparing two tellings shows how perspective shapes the story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A narrator in a comedy brags, “Nobody noticed my mistake,” while {n} people point and laugh. What effect does this create?","choices":["Humour, because we see what he cannot","Suspense about a mystery","Sadness, because he is honest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who knows the truth: the reader or the narrator?","explain":"The gap between his boast and what we see makes the scene funny. Unreliable narrators often create humour and irony this way.","params":{"n":[4,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which question best tells third limited from omniscient?","choices":["Whose thoughts does the narrator reveal?","Does the narrator say he or she?","Is the story set in the past?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both use he or she. What else differs?","explain":"Both kinds use he or she. Limited reveals one mind, while omniscient can reveal many, so ask whose thoughts we get."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A murder story is told by a narrator who leaves out one key night. Why should readers notice?","choices":["Gaps can be a clue that the narrator is hiding something","Gaps mean the author made an error","Gaps prove the narrator is omniscient"],"answer":0,"hint":"Unreliable narrators can mislead by what they skip.","explain":"Leaving things out is a classic sign of an unreliable narrator. Agatha Christie's The Murder of Roger Ackroyd hides its twist in a gap like this."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"“I opened the door and froze. Someone had been in my room.” Which perspective is this?","choices":["First person","Third person limited","Omniscient"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the narrator's pronoun. Is the teller inside the story saying I?","explain":"The narrator says I and takes part in the events, so this is first person. We only know what this one character sees and thinks."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A narrator follows only Ella. We hear her thoughts, but in chapter {n} we never learn what her rival is planning. Which perspective is this?","choices":["Third person limited","Omniscient","First person"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the minds we enter. One mind, or many?","explain":"The narrator uses she and stays with Ella's thoughts only. Because we are locked out of the rival's mind, this is third person limited, not omniscient.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In a film, we see a monster hiding under the bed. The girl climbs in, smiling. Why do we feel tense?","choices":["We know something she does not: dramatic irony","The story has a surprise twist","The narrator is lying to us"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who knows about the monster: us, her, or both?","explain":"We know about the danger and she does not. That gap in knowledge is dramatic irony, and it creates tension now rather than a surprise later."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Jay narrates: “I never cheat.” Later, {n} classmates see answers written on his hand. What does this suggest?","choices":["Jay is an unreliable narrator","Jay is telling the truth","The author is a cheat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare what Jay says with what others see.","explain":"Jay's claim clashes with the evidence, so we should not trust his account. Readers spot unreliable narrators by testing words against actions and other characters' reactions.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why might an author choose a limited narrator for a mystery?","choices":["To hide what other characters know, building suspense","To tell the reader the answer early","Because limited narrators cannot use he or she"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a limited view keep from the reader?","explain":"A limited narrator only knows what one character knows. The reader is kept in the dark with them, so clues and suspects stay uncertain until the end."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every story has a narrator, the voice telling it. Ask two questions. Who says I? Whose thoughts do we get? If the narrator says I and takes part, it is first person. If the narrator says he or she, it is third person.","Third person comes in two kinds. Third limited follows one character. We hear only their thoughts, so we miss what they miss. Omniscient means all-knowing. The narrator can step into any mind and tell us things no character knows.","This choice shapes how we feel. A limited view keeps us guessing, which builds suspense. When we know more than a character, we get dramatic irony. We want to shout a warning, but they cannot hear us.","Some narrators cannot be trusted. They may lie, forget or not understand. This is an unreliable narrator. Look for clues: actions that clash with their words, other people reacting oddly, or details they skip. The narrator is not the author. It is a voice the author made."],"adult":["This topic covers point of view in fiction: first person, third person limited and third person omniscient. It also covers the unreliable narrator. Learners explain how each choice widens or narrows what the reader knows. They then link that to effects such as suspense, dramatic irony and humour.","The lab uses one small scene: a smashed vase, a boy called Sam, his gran and a cat. The same event is told by Sam, from Gran's view and by an all-knowing narrator. Learners watch which thought bubbles open. Then they name the view in three short passages, find the facts Gran does not know, and catch Sam's account out against the evidence.","The most common mistake is to treat third person as all-knowing. The test is not the pronoun but how many minds we enter. Pupils also mix up dramatic irony, where we already know, with a twist, which surprises us later. Many trust a first-person narrator because they say so. Some also treat the narrator as the author.","At home, read the opening of any novel together and ask: whose head are we in, and what can we not know yet? Classic unreliable narrators to try at this age include Christopher in The Curious Incident, a reliable but limited teller, and Poe's narrator in The Tell-Tale Heart, who insists he is sane."]},"familyTip":"Tell a small family event, such as who ate the last biscuit, twice: once as yourself saying I, and once as an all-knowing narrator. Ask what the second telling reveals that the first could not.","keyIdea":"Who tells a story decides what the reader can know. First person gives one mind from inside, third limited follows one mind from outside, and omniscient opens every mind. Narrators can also be wrong or lie, so test what they say against what happens.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which perspective is it? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Third limited","Omniscient"],"items":[["We hear only Ella's thoughts",0],["We miss what the rival plans",0],["We learn as the hero learns",0],["We hear Tom's and Mum's thoughts",1],["We know a secret no character knows",1],["We jump between minds in a scene",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnarr","caption":"Flagship lab: tell one event three ways, name the view, find what Gran doesn't know, catch Sam's lies and mix narrators.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ak_ZgoMKRQ":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What must be true of the words inside quotation marks?","choices":["They are exactly the writer's words","They mean the same as the writer's words","They are the most important words in the text"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quotation marks make a promise to the reader. What is it?","explain":"Quotation marks promise the writer's exact words. Words that only mean the same are a paraphrase, so they go outside the marks."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The text says: \"Ana hid her face and would not speak.\" Which quote best proves Ana was shy?","choices":["\"hid her face and would not speak\"","\"Ana\"","\"Ana was shy and quiet\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the copied words that show the feeling.","explain":"\"hid her face and would not speak\" is exact and proves it. One name proves nothing, and the text never says \"Ana was shy and quiet\"."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The text never says \"Leo was hungry\". Which quote could prove it?","choices":["\"Leo's tummy rumbled as he stared at the cake\"","\"Leo was hungry\"","\"Leo walked home\""],"answer":0,"hint":"You can quote a clue and infer the feeling from it.","explain":"A rumbling tummy and staring at cake are clues that let you infer hunger. You cannot quote words the text never uses."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The text says: \"She ran as fast as the wind.\" Kim writes: She \"ran as quick as the wind.\" What is wrong?","choices":["She changed \"fast\" to \"quick\"","Nothing, it means the same","She used too few words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare Kim's words with the text one at a time.","explain":"\"quick\" is not the writer's word. Even when the meaning stays the same, a changed word makes it a misquote."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where do the quotation marks go in: The poet says the sea was \"grey and cold\" that morning?","choices":["Round only the copied words \"grey and cold\"","Round the whole sentence","Round \"The poet says\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words were copied from the poem?","explain":"Only \"grey and cold\" came from the poem, so only those words go inside the marks. The rest is your own sentence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What should usually come after a quotation in your answer?","choices":["A few words that say what it shows","Another quotation straight away","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"How will the reader know why you chose those words?","explain":"Explaining what the quote shows links it to your point, for example \"which shows that Mia was nervous\"."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Max copies a {a}-word sentence but changes {b} of the words. How many words does he need to fix to quote it accurately?","answer":"b","hint":"An accurate quote has every word exactly as the writer wrote it.","explain":"All {b} changed words must go back to the writer's words. One changed word is enough to make a misquote.","params":{"a":[8,14],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"An article says: \"Owls can turn their heads a long way round.\" Which quote proves owls can see behind them?","choices":["\"Owls can turn their heads a long way round\"","\"Owls are birds\"","\"Owls can see behind their backs\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Choose words that are really in the article and that support the point.","explain":"Turning the head a long way round is the clue. The article never says \"see behind their backs\", and \"Owls are birds\" is not in it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Your answer quotes {a} separate phrases from the text. How many opening quotation marks (“) does it need?","answer":"a","hint":"Each quoted phrase needs its own pair of marks: one to open and one to close.","explain":"Each of the {a} phrases opens with its own “ mark, so you need {a} opening marks, and {a} closing marks too.","params":{"a":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The story says: \"Tom slammed the door and stomped up the stairs.\" Which quote best proves Tom was angry?","choices":["\"slammed the door and stomped\"","\"up the stairs\"","\"Tom was very cross\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words show how Tom acted? Are they the story's own words?","explain":"\"slammed the door and stomped\" is copied exactly and shows anger. \"up the stairs\" does not show a feeling, and the story never says \"Tom was very cross\"."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A quote you want to use has {a} words, but only the last {b} prove your point. How many words should you quote?","answer":"b","hint":"Quote only the words that do the proving.","explain":"Quote just the {b} words that prove the point. The other words only hide your proof.","params":{"a":[12,20],"b":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The text says: \"The river was wide and brown.\" Which is an accurate quote?","choices":["The text says the river \"was wide and brown.\"","The text says the river \"was big and brown.\"","The text says the river \"is wide and brown.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare each word with the text, one at a time.","explain":"Only \"was wide and brown\" matches exactly. \"big\" changes a word, and \"is\" changes the ending of the verb."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam's sentence has {a} words. Only {b} of them are copied from the text. How many words go inside the quotation marks?","answer":"b","hint":"Only copied words go inside the marks. Your own words stay outside.","explain":"The {b} copied words go inside the marks. The other words are Sam's own, so they stay outside.","params":{"a":[10,16],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is a signal phrase that leads in to a quotation?","choices":["According to the text,","I think that","In my opinion,"],"answer":0,"hint":"A signal phrase points the reader to the text, not to you.","explain":"\"According to the text,\" tells the reader the next words come from the text. The other two lead in to your own opinion."}],"explain":{"kid":["When you write about a text, you can prove your point with its own words. That is a quotation. Copy the words exactly, then put quotation marks round them. Your own words stay outside the marks.","Quote just the words that prove your point. To show Mia was nervous, \"Her hands shook\" is enough. A whole paragraph hides your proof, and one word on its own may not make sense.","Check your copy word by word. \"Her hand was shaking\" is not what the story says, even if it means the same. Changing a word, an ending or a small word like \"her\" turns a quote into a misquote.","Texts do not always say things outright. The story never says Mia was proud, but \"Mia grinned all the way home\" is a clue. Quote the clue, then say what it shows. Start with a signal phrase, such as \"According to the text,\"."],"adult":["This reading skill (ages 10 to 11, Common Core RL.5.1 and RI.5.1) asks learners to quote accurately from literary and informational texts, both for what a text says explicitly and for inferences. In practice that means choosing relevant words, copying them exactly and punctuating them as a quotation.","Good quotations are short and relevant: a phrase or sentence that proves one point. They are introduced with a signal phrase (\"The author states\", \"According to the text\") and followed by an explanation that links the words to the claim. Copied words go inside quotation marks; the learner's own wording stays outside.","Common mistakes: copying a long passage instead of the proving words; paraphrasing but still using quotation marks; changing small words or endings (hand for hands, the for her); quoting accurate words that do not support the claim; and dropping a quotation in with no signal phrase or explanation.","In the lab, learners drag across a story to select proof for a claim, spot the three changes in a classmate's misquote, tap gaps to place opening and closing marks, quote a clue for an inference, and build a signal phrase, quotation and explanation from tiles. The challenge uses a new non-fiction text about honeybees."]},"familyTip":"When your child tells you a book character is mean or brave, ask, \"Which words in the book tell you that?\" Let them find the exact words and read them out, then say what those words show.","keyIdea":"A quotation is the writer's exact words, copied word for word and put inside quotation marks. Quote only the words that prove your point, whether the text says it outright or gives a clue you infer from. Lead in with a signal phrase such as \"According to the text,\" and then explain what the quotation shows.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"The story says: \"Her hands shook as she held her poem.\" Is each one an accurate quote? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Accurate quote","Not accurate"],"items":[["\"Her hands shook\"",0],["\"held her poem\"",0],["\"as she held her poem\"",0],["\"Her hand shook\"",1],["\"she held the poem\"",1],["\"Her hands were shaking\"",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagquote","caption":"Flagship lab: drag across a story to quote the proof, spot a misquote, place quotation marks, quote a clue and build a signal phrase, quote and explanation.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_aFvsj35QzC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A page says: Bats sleep in the day. Bats fly at night. They eat bugs. What is it about?","choices":["Bats","Bugs","The day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is \"they\"? What does every sentence tell about?","explain":"They means bats, so every sentence tells about bats. Bugs are one detail."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which sentence sums up a page about how a chick hatches?","choices":["This page is about how a chick hatches.","Chicks are fluffy.","This page is about farms."],"answer":0,"hint":"A summary tells about the whole page, not one detail.","explain":"The first sentence covers the whole page. Fluffy is one detail, and farms is too wide."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A book about cars has {n} facts. One says \"Bikes have two wheels.\" Does it belong?","choices":["No, it is about bikes","Yes, bikes are like cars","Yes, it has wheels"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: is this fact about cars, or something else?","explain":"Bikes are close to cars, but the fact tells about bikes, so it does not belong with the {n} car facts.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Is \"My dog is the best\" a key fact for a page about how dogs smell things?","choices":["No, it is what someone thinks","Yes, it says dog","Yes, it is the topic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it teach how dogs smell things?","explain":"It says what someone thinks. It does not teach how dogs smell, so it is not a key fact."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You read a page with {n} facts about sharks. How many key facts might you retell?","choices":["Two or three","None","Just the title"],"answer":0,"hint":"A retell gives a few of the most useful facts.","explain":"Pick two or three key facts from the {n} to retell. You do not need all of them, but you need some.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The sun came out. The ice melted. Which word links them?","choices":["so","but","or"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the sun make the ice melt?","explain":"The sun made the ice melt, so we use so: the sun came out, so the ice melted."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A page says: Owls hunt at night. Owls eat mice. Owls have big eyes. A child says it is about mice. What went wrong?","choices":["Mice is only one detail","Mice is the main topic","Nothing went wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many sentences tell about mice?","explain":"Mice is in one sentence. Every sentence tells about owls, so owls is the main topic."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A page says: Ants live in big groups. Ants dig tunnels. Ants carry food home. What is the main topic?","choices":["Ants","Tunnels","Food"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does every sentence tell about?","explain":"Every sentence tells about ants. Tunnels and food are details about the ants."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A fact book about the moon has {n} pages. Which fact belongs in it?","choices":["The moon goes round the Earth.","Cars have four wheels.","I like my red hat."],"answer":0,"hint":"A fact belongs when it tells about the topic of the book.","explain":"Only the first fact tells about the moon, so it belongs in all {n} pages of the moon book.","params":{"n":[8,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is a key fact about how fish breathe?","choices":["Fish use gills to take air from water.","My fish is called Bob.","I think fish are fun."],"answer":0,"hint":"A key fact teaches about the topic. It is not what someone likes or owns.","explain":"Gills taking air from water is the fact that teaches how fish breathe. The others are about someone."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"It snowed for {n} days. Which is the best way to link it to the next fact?","choices":["So the roads were white.","So it was very hot.","So I like jam."],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick what snow could make happen.","explain":"After {n} days of snow, the roads were white. Snow does not make it hot, and jam has no link.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A fact page tells you about one big thing. That is the main topic. Ask: what does every sentence tell about?","Owls hunt at night. Owls have big eyes. Owls eat mice. Mice are in it, but just once. Every sentence is about owls. So the main topic is owls.","Key facts teach you about the topic. \"Plants need water\" is a key fact. \"I like sunflowers best\" is not. It just says what someone likes.","Some facts are linked. It rained all day, so the river got high. The word so shows how one thing made the next thing happen."],"adult":["Informational texts, such as fact books about animals or space, are organised around a main topic. At five to seven, children learn to say what a short text is mostly about in one sentence, to list two or three key facts they learned, and to explain how two facts or events are connected, often by cause and effect.","The lab builds each skill with its own action. Children tap a word to see which sentences tell about it, sort facts into a shark book, pick the one sentence that sums up a page, choose key facts about plants while leaving out opinions, and link pairs of facts with so.","Common mistakes to watch for: naming a detail that is vivid or comes last, such as mice on an owl page, as the topic; accepting a fact because it is near the topic, such as dolphins in a shark book; giving one fact as a summary; and treating opinions or personal stories as key details. Pronouns can also hide the topic, so check what they or it points back to.","To help, after reading a page together, ask: \"What was every sentence about?\" then \"Tell me two things you learned.\" Use the title and pictures as clues, but check them against the sentences."]},"familyTip":"After reading a non-fiction page or a cereal box together, ask, \"What was it all about?\" and \"Tell me two things you learned.\" Then ask how two facts are linked: \"It was cold, so what happened?\"","keyIdea":"A fact book or page has a main topic: the one thing every sentence tells about. Facts that tell about the topic are key details; things someone likes are not. You can say the main topic in one sentence, retell a few key facts, and use words like so to show how two facts are linked.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"A page about frogs: is each line a key fact? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🐸 Key fact","💭 Not a key fact"],"items":[["Frogs lay eggs in water",0],["Tadpoles grow legs",0],["Frogs catch flies with their tongues",0],["Frogs can jump far",0],["I think frogs are cute",1],["My pal has a fish",1],["I had toast today",1],["Green is my best colour",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtopic","caption":"Flagship lab: find what every sentence is about, sort facts into a shark book, pick a one-sentence summary, choose key facts and link facts with so.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XeMZdf2Y9W":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is on the front cover of a book?","choices":["The title and a big picture","The blurb","Only page numbers"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you see first when you pick up a book?","explain":"The front cover shows the title and a big picture. The blurb is on the back cover."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Who writes the words in a book?","choices":["The author","The illustrator","The spine"],"answer":0,"hint":"One name is for the words, one is for the pictures.","explain":"The author writes the words. The illustrator draws the pictures. The spine is a part of the book, not a person."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where can you find the title, the author and the illustrator together?","choices":["The title page","The back cover","The last page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Open the book. Which page near the front names the people who made it?","explain":"The title page, just inside the book, names the title, the author and the illustrator."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Point: Trees are important. Which is a reason?","choices":["Trees give us clean air","Some trees are tall","I climbed a tree"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it show trees matter? A true fact is not always a reason.","explain":"Clean air shows trees are important. Being tall is true, but it does not show trees matter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A page about frogs has {n} sentences. Which one backs up \"Frogs need water\"?","choices":["Their skin must stay wet","Frogs are green","I like frogs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which sentence tells you why frogs need water?","explain":"Of the {n} sentences, \"their skin must stay wet\" is the reason. Green is a fact that does not explain it, and liking frogs is an opinion.","params":{"n":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"An author gives {n} reasons that bees help us. What do the reasons do?","choices":["Back up the point","Name the illustrator","Tell you the page number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why does an author add reasons after a point?","explain":"All {n} reasons back up the point that bees help us. They show the point is right.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which sentence uses because to join a point and a reason?","choices":["We brush our teeth because it stops holes","We brush our teeth because I have a red brush","We brush our teeth and it is Monday"],"answer":0,"hint":"The part after because must show why the point is right.","explain":"Stopping holes is why we brush our teeth. A red brush and Monday do not explain the point."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where is the blurb on a book?","choices":["On the back cover","On the front cover","On the spine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turn the book over. Where is the short bit that tells you what the book is about?","explain":"The blurb is on the back cover. It tells you what the book is about, so you can choose if you want to read it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A book has {n} pictures. Who drew them?","choices":["The illustrator","The author","The reader"],"answer":0,"hint":"One person writes the words. Another person can draw the pictures. Which name is for the pictures?","explain":"The illustrator drew all {n} pictures. The author wrote the words. You find both names on the title page.","params":{"n":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Point: Dogs are good helpers. Which is a reason?","choices":["Dogs can guide people who cannot see","Dogs have four legs","I saw a dog in the park"],"answer":0,"hint":"A reason shows the point is right. Which one shows dogs help people?","explain":"Guiding people who cannot see shows dogs help. Four legs is true but does not show helping, and seeing a dog is not a reason."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {n} books on a shelf. Which part of each book can you see?","choices":["The spine","The blurb","The title page"],"answer":0,"hint":"On a shelf, books stand side by side. Which part faces out?","explain":"On a shelf you see each spine, so all {n} spines show their titles. The blurb is on the back and the title page is inside.","params":{"n":[6,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at a book. The front cover is what you see first. It has the title and a big picture. The spine is the thin side. On a shelf, you see the spine.","Turn the book over. That is the back cover. The short bit of writing there is the blurb. It tells you what the book is about.","Open the book. The title page has the title again. It names the author, who wrote the words. It names the illustrator, who drew the pictures.","Books that teach facts make a point, like \"Bees are helpful.\" Then they give reasons. \"Bees make honey\" is a reason. \"Bees have six legs\" is true, but it does not show bees help us. So it is not a reason for that point."],"adult":["Five to eight is when children move from enjoying books to knowing how they work. This topic covers the parts of a book (front cover, back cover, spine, blurb and title page), the roles named on the title page (author and illustrator), and an early reading-for-meaning skill from information texts: finding the reasons an author gives to support a point.","In the US Common Core these are standards RI.K.5 and RI.K.6 (parts of a book, author and illustrator) and RI.1.8 and RI.2.8 (identify the reasons an author gives to support points). In England they sit in Year 1 and Year 2 reading: discussing the title and how items of information are related, and learning how non-fiction books are structured. A point is the main claim; a reason is a sentence that shows the claim is right.","Common mistakes to watch for: mixing up the author and the illustrator; looking for the blurb on the front cover; calling any true fact a reason even when it does not support the point, such as \"bees have six legs\" for \"bees are helpful\"; and offering an opinion such as \"I like frogs\" as a reason. Be careful to praise a true fact before explaining why it does not back up this point.","In the lab your child drags labels onto a book's front cover, spine, back cover and blurb, taps the title, author and illustrator on a title page, picks the three sentences that show bees are helpful, sorts dog facts under the point they support, and builds point because reason sentences."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, before you read, ask your child to find the front cover, the spine and the blurb, then the author and illustrator on the title page. With an information book, read one page and ask, \"What is the author's point? Which sentence backs it up?\" Use the word because: \"Bees are helpful because they make honey.\"","keyIdea":"A book has a front cover with the title and a picture, a spine down the side, and a back cover with the blurb, which tells you what the book is about. Inside, the title page names the title, the author, who writes the words, and the illustrator, who draws the pictures. In an information book, the author makes a point and gives reasons that back it up. A reason shows the point is right; a true fact that does not show this is not a reason.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Point: bees are helpful. Is each line a reason for it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Reason bees help","❌ Not a reason"],"items":[["Bees make honey",0],["Bees carry pollen",0],["Bees help fruit grow",0],["Bees help flowers make seeds",0],["Bees have six legs",1],["Bees are stripy",1],["I like yellow",1],["My cat sleeps a lot",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbook","caption":"Flagship lab: label a book's covers, spine and blurb, find the title, author and illustrator, then spot and sort an author's reasons.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OltpfaX7l6":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is a push word that shows persuading?","choices":["should","river","Monday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word tells someone what to do?","explain":"\"Should\" tells you what to do, so it is a push word. River and Monday just name things."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"A dragon ate the king's hat and burped out a rainbow.\" Why was it written?","choices":["To entertain","To inform","To persuade"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it true? Is anyone asked to do something?","explain":"It is a silly, made-up story with no facts and no push words, so it was written to entertain."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"\"The school wasted {n} pounds on new paint.\" How does the author feel?","choices":["Against the paint","For the paint","We cannot tell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the word wasted. Is it a happy or a cross word?","explain":"\"Wasted\" shows the author thinks the {n} pounds were spent badly. They are against it.","params":{"n":[200,900]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You think the author's purpose is to persuade. How do you prove it?","choices":["Point to the push words in the text","Say you liked it","Count the pictures"],"answer":0,"hint":"Proof comes from the words the author wrote.","explain":"Point to words like \"you must\" or \"best ever\". The text itself is your evidence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A poster says the zoo has {n} animals and you should visit this summer. What is the main purpose?","choices":["To persuade","To inform","To entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"The number is a fact, but what does the poster want you to do?","explain":"\"You should visit\" is a push. The {n} animals are a fact used to persuade you to go.","params":{"n":[50,400]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the author's point of view?","choices":["What the author thinks about the topic","The page the author wrote on","What every reader must think"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the author's opinion.","explain":"Point of view is what the author thinks. Readers can have a different view of their own."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"Penguins cannot fly, but they swim very fast.\" Why was it written?","choices":["To inform","To persuade","To entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it true? Does it ask you to do anything?","explain":"It gives true facts about penguins and asks nothing of you, so the author wants to inform."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"Get your tickets now! It is the best show in town!\" Why was it written?","choices":["To persuade","To inform","To entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it push you to do something? Look for now and best.","explain":"\"Get your tickets now\" tells you what to do and \"best\" praises the show, so the author wants to persuade you."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A fact book says a blue whale can be {n} metres long. Why was it written?","choices":["To inform","To persuade","To entertain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it teaching you a true fact, or asking you to do something?","explain":"A length of {n} metres is a true fact that teaches you about whales. The author wants to inform.","params":{"n":[24,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"An advert says: \"{n} out of 10 kids love Zoom Shoes. Buy yours today!\" What is the purpose?","choices":["To persuade, using a fact as help","To inform, because it has a number","To entertain, because it is fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the push words. Is the number there to teach you, or to make you buy?","explain":"\"Buy yours today\" pushes you. The fact that {n} out of 10 kids love them is there to make the push stronger.","params":{"n":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The author thinks dogs make the best pets. You like cats more. What is true?","choices":["Your view and the author's view can be different","You must agree with the author","The author is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a reader have to think the same as the author?","explain":"The author's point of view is what the author thinks. You can disagree: the author thinks dogs are best, but I think cats are."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every text has a reason. Authors write to persuade you, to inform you, or to entertain you. That reason is called the author's purpose.","To persuade is to make you think or do something. Look for push words like should, must and best. An advert says: You must try it! Persuaders often add facts to make their case stronger.","To inform is to teach you true facts, like how owls hunt. To entertain is to give you a story or a joke to enjoy.","The author's point of view is what the author thinks. You do not have to agree. You can say: The author thinks school should start later, but I think it should not. Words show how the author feels: wasted money sounds cross, spent money sounds calm."],"adult":["Identifying the author's purpose is a core reading-comprehension standard: US Common Core RI.2.6 asks children to identify the main purpose of a text, and RI.3.6 asks them to distinguish their own point of view from that of the author. The English national curriculum for Years 3 to 6 adds distinguishing statements of fact from opinion and explaining how language contributes to meaning.","Teachers often use the shorthand PIE: persuade, inform, entertain. Informational texts usually inform, adverts and letters to the editor persuade, and stories and poems entertain, but form is only a clue. The best evidence is the language: imperatives, 'should' and 'must', and superlatives signal persuasion.","Common mistakes: deciding that any text with facts must be informing (persuaders use facts as evidence); thinking that a fun or colourful text must be entertaining when it is selling something; believing that disagreeing with the author means you read it wrong; and naming a purpose without quoting the words that show it.","Attitude is shown through word choice. 'The council wasted money' and 'The council invested money' report the same fact, but the loaded verb tells you which side the author is on. Ask your child to swap one word and say how the author now seems to feel."]},"familyTip":"Pick up any leaflet, cereal box or news headline at home. Ask: Why was this written? Which words tell you? Then ask what your child thinks, and let them disagree with the author.","keyIdea":"Every text has an author's purpose: to persuade you, to inform you, or to entertain you. Push words such as should, must and best show an author is persuading, even when the text also uses facts. The author's point of view is what the author thinks; your own view can be different. Loaded words show the author's attitude, and you prove a purpose by pointing to the words that show it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Why was each text written? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📢 Persuade","📘 Inform","🎭 Entertain"],"items":[["You must try our new pizza!",0],["Vote for a longer lunch break!",0],["Frogs lay eggs called spawn.",1],["The Moon goes round the Earth.",1],["The cat wore boots and sang.",2],["A giant sneezed and the town flew away.",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpurp","caption":"Flagship lab: sort texts by purpose, find push words, pin your view and the author's, swap loaded words, and prove each purpose.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_RjZNxkQb85":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the context of reception?","choices":["The time, place and values of whoever reads or watches the text","The time and place where the author wrote it","The plot events that come before a scene"],"answer":0,"hint":"Reception means receiving: who is receiving the text?","explain":"The context of reception is the time, place and values of the reader or audience, at any point. The author's world is the context of production."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"'Shakespeare was sexist because he did not support votes for women.' What is the slip?","choices":["Presentism: judging the past by a modern standard","It uses too much evidence","It confuses comedy with tragedy"],"answer":0,"hint":"When did campaigns for women's votes begin?","explain":"Votes for women were not a live issue in the 1590s, so this judges the past by a much later standard. That slip is called presentism."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why is 'Elizabethan audiences found the ending funny' a weak claim?","choices":["It treats a whole audience as having one reaction","Elizabethans never watched comedies","The ending is not in the play"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did every person in the theatre react the same way?","explain":"It assumes one audience mind. Hedge it: 'some original audiences may have laughed', especially as Fletcher's sequel shows others resisted."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Jean Rhys's Wide Sargasso Sea retells Jane Eyre from Bertha's point of view. It is best described as...","choices":["a later reinterpretation shaped by its own context","proof of what Charlotte Brontë intended","the context of production of Jane Eyre"],"answer":0,"hint":"It was written in 1966, over a century after Jane Eyre.","explain":"Written in 1966, it reads Jane Eyre from a postcolonial position. That makes it a reception, not evidence of Brontë's intent."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"In a {n}-mark essay, what should context do?","choices":["Sharpen an argument about the text","Replace close reading of quotations","Fill the first paragraph with background"],"answer":0,"hint":"Examiners reward context that is linked to the text.","explain":"In a {n}-mark essay, context earns credit when it changes how a line or choice is read. It supports close reading and never replaces it.","params":{"n":[25,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A director in {y} stages Katherine's speech as bitter irony. What does this show?","choices":["Reception can reshape how a text's meaning is presented","The words of the speech were rewritten","The play has no meaning of its own"],"answer":0,"hint":"The words are the same. What changed?","explain":"The {y} staging keeps the words but changes delivery and framing. That shows reception shaping meaning, while the text still limits what is plausible.","params":{"y":[1980,2024]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Reading 'Thy husband is thy lord' against the 1563 Homily on matrimony is which kind of reading?","choices":["Historicist","Performance-based","Purely personal response"],"answer":0,"hint":"It reads the line beside a document from the same era.","explain":"Setting the line beside a document from its own time is a historicist reading of the context of production."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a context of production for The Taming of the Shrew?","choices":["Women's roles were played by boy actors","A modern audience's views on control in relationships","A film version made in 1929"],"answer":0,"hint":"Production is the world the play was first made and staged in.","explain":"Boy actors playing women belongs to how the play was first made and staged in the 1590s, so it is production. The other two are later audiences: reception."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A reader in {y} finds Katherine's final speech disturbing. Which context mainly explains this?","choices":["The context of reception","The context of production","The play's source material"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whose world is shaping the response: the writer's or the reader's?","explain":"A reaction in {y} comes from that reader's own values and time, which is the context of reception.","params":{"y":[1990,2026]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence uses context to sharpen an argument?","choices":["Under coverture a wife's goods became her husband's, so 'my goods, my chattels' states the law, not just a boast","Shakespeare was born in Stratford in 1564","The Elizabethan era was a time of great change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one changes how you read a specific line?","explain":"The coverture sentence ties a fact to a quotation and changes how we read it. The others are true or vague but change nothing about the play."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Fletcher answered the play in a sequel about {n} years after it was written. What does that show?","choices":["Early audiences did not all accept the taming","Fletcher misunderstood Shakespeare","The play was forgotten until the 20th century"],"answer":0,"hint":"If someone writes a reply, did everyone agree with the original?","explain":"A sequel in which Petruchio is tamed, written only about {n} years later, shows the taming was argued with in its own era. So a resistant reading is not purely modern.","params":{"n":[17,21]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A play is written once, but it is read again and again. The world the writer lived in is the context of production. The time and place of the person reading or watching is the context of reception.","Take Katherine's last speech in The Taming of the Shrew. She tells wives to obey their husbands. The words have not changed since the 1590s. But audiences have. Around 1611 John Fletcher wrote a sequel where Petruchio is tamed himself. In 1929 Mary Pickford winked at the camera as she spoke the lines. Many modern stagings play the speech as irony, or as a woman worn down.","So meaning is not only in the words. It is made when a reader meets them. That does not mean any reading goes. You still need evidence from the text.","Use context to change how a line reads. Coverture meant a wife's goods became her husband's. So when Petruchio calls Kate 'my goods, my chattels', he is stating the law. That fact sharpens your point. A date you just drop in does not."],"adult":["A-level English Literature tests context directly. AQA, Edexcel and OCR all use Assessment Objective 3 for it. Students must show how the contexts in which texts are written and received shape meaning. Strong answers split the two. The context of production is the writer's era, genre, theatre, law and beliefs. The context of reception is any later audience, including the student.","The Taming of the Shrew is a classic test case. Within about 20 years, Fletcher's The Woman's Prize, or The Tamer Tamed, answered it. Garrick's 1754 version kept the taming as farce. The 1929 Pickford film added a wink. Modern stagings often unsettle the speech. Reception theory, from Jauss's horizon of expectations to reader-response criticism, explains why the same words yield new readings.","Common mistakes: bolting on facts about the writer's life that change nothing about a line. Presentism, which judges a 1590s text by a modern standard. Saying 'Elizabethans thought' as if a whole audience had one mind. And letting context replace close reading. A careful answer hedges with 'may have' or 'some audiences', ties each fact to a quotation, and keeps the text at the centre.","In the lab your teenager sorts six contexts into production or reception. Next they place four real responses to Katherine's speech on a timeline. Then they judge whether context sentences sharpen a point or are bolted on. Finally they match one line to historicist, feminist and performance readings, and catch the classic slips."]},"familyTip":"Watch two short clips of the same scene from different productions or films together and ask: what did each director want the audience to feel, and what about their own time might explain the choice?","keyIdea":"Every text has two kinds of context. The context of production is the world it was made in: the writer's time, place, laws, beliefs and theatre. The context of reception is the time, place and values of each reader or audience. The words stay the same, but readers in different times and places bring different values, so they can read the same text in different ways. Good essays use context to sharpen an argument about the text, never to replace it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each one a context of production or of reception? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🖋️ Production","👓 Reception"],"items":[["Boy actors played women",0],["Coverture law",0],["The 1563 marriage homily",0],["Genre: Elizabethan comedy",0],["A 2020s audience",1],["Garrick's 1754 version",1],["Pickford's 1929 film",1],["Your own reading today",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagctx","caption":"Flagship lab: split production and reception, date four audiences, judge context use, match lenses, spot slips.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_V6456X6pJE":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which statement is a fact?","choices":["Bats are mammals.","Bats are creepy.","Bats should be kept as pets."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could a scientist check?","explain":"\"Bats are mammals\" is a fact: bats feed their young on milk. Creepy is a feeling, and should is a view about what ought to happen."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"Our town has {n} parks, and they are all beautiful.\" What is this sentence?","choices":["A fact and an opinion","Just a fact","Just an opinion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Split it into two parts. Check each part on its own.","explain":"\"Our town has {n} parks\" can be counted, so it is a fact. \"They are all beautiful\" is a view. The sentence holds both.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"\"Everyone knows that cats are better than dogs.\" Fact or opinion?","choices":["Opinion","Fact, because everyone knows it","Fact, because it is about animals"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is \"better\" something you can measure?","explain":"It is an opinion. \"Everyone knows\" is a trick to make a view sound certain, and better is a judgement."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is \"Water freezes at 0 °C\" a fact?","choices":["It can be checked with a thermometer","Most people believe it","It is written in a sure way"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes any statement a fact?","explain":"It can be tested with ice and a thermometer. Belief and a confident tone do not make a fact; being checkable does."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word turns this into an opinion? \"The match lasted {n} minutes and was thrilling.\"","choices":["thrilling","lasted","minutes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word tells you how the writer felt?","explain":"Thrilling is the judging word. The {n} minutes can be checked with a clock, so that part is a fact.","params":{"n":[60,95]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"\"Venus is the hottest planet in our solar system.\" Fact or opinion?","choices":["Fact","Opinion, because hottest is a judging word","Opinion, because it is surprising"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hottest here is measured with instruments, not felt.","explain":"It is a fact. Space probes measured Venus at about 465 °C, hotter than Mercury. Hottest is a measurement here, not a taste."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A report says: \"Clearly, the new road is a terrible idea.\" What does \"clearly\" do?","choices":["Makes a view sound certain","Turns the view into a fact","Shows the writer checked it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the writer give any evidence?","explain":"Clearly makes the view sound sure. Terrible is still a judgement, and nothing was checked."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"The school trip costs £{n}.\" Is this a fact or an opinion?","choices":["Fact","Opinion","Both"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could you check it? Look at the letter from school or ask the office.","explain":"It is a fact. You can check the price of £{n} on the letter. There is no judging word.","params":{"n":[5,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"\"The {n} best songs of the year are all by one band.\" Fact or opinion?","choices":["Opinion","Fact","Both"],"answer":0,"hint":"The number {n} can be counted. But is \"best\" something you can check?","explain":"It is an opinion. \"Best\" is a judgement, and the number {n} does not change that. Another listener would pick different songs.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word makes this an opinion? \"The museum, which opened in 1990, is wonderful.\"","choices":["wonderful","opened","1990"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word tells you what the writer thinks?","explain":"Wonderful is the judging word. \"Opened in 1990\" can be checked, so that part is a fact."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"An advert says: \"It is a fact that Zing is the tastiest drink.\" What is it?","choices":["An opinion dressed as a fact","A fact, because it says so","A fact, because many people buy it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can anyone measure \"tastiest\"?","explain":"It is an opinion. Saying \"it is a fact that\" does not make tastiest checkable. Taste is a judgement."}],"explain":{"kid":["Ask one question: could I check it? \"A blue whale can grow to about 30 metres long\" can be checked in a book or with a tape. That is a fact. \"Whales are the most amazing animals\" cannot. Someone else may love tigers more. That is an opinion.","Look for judging words. Best, worst, brilliant, boring, should and delicious all tell you what someone thinks. A number does not make a sentence a fact. \"The top five goals were all spectacular\" still has the judging word spectacular.","A surprising fact is still a fact. Sharks were on Earth before trees. It sounds odd, but fossils let scientists check it. A fact can also turn out to be wrong when someone checks it. It is still the kind of statement you can test.","Writers mix the two. \"Our school has 400 pupils, so it is far too crowded\" has a fact, then a view. Adverts go further. They say \"it is a fact that\" or \"everyone knows\", or use sure words like clearly. None of that makes a view checkable."],"adult":["The English national curriculum for Years 5 and 6 asks pupils to tell statements of fact from opinion when they read. Here they label each statement in a non-fiction text. They explain how they can tell. They also spot tricks that make a view look like a fact.","The test is: can it be checked? A fact can be measured, counted, seen or looked up in a source you trust. An opinion is a judgement, a feeling or a belief, so fair people can disagree. Judging words (best, brilliant), words like should and must, and phrases like I think are the usual signs.","Common mistakes: thinking any sentence with a number is a fact; calling a strange or new fact an opinion; thinking a view held by lots of people becomes a fact; labelling a whole sentence when only one part judges; and trusting an advert that says \"it is a fact that\". Another slip is calling a false claim an opinion. A false claim can still be checked. It just fails the check.","In the lab the learner swipes statements into fact and opinion, taps the judging word in four sentences, and colours the fact part and the view part of mixed sentences. Then they tag three advert tricks and colour a whole news report."]},"keyIdea":"A fact is a statement that can be checked: measured, counted, observed or looked up in a reliable source. An opinion is what someone thinks, feels or believes, so it cannot be proved. Judging words such as best, brilliant, should and delicious mark an opinion, and one sentence can hold a fact and an opinion together. Writers, especially in adverts, often dress an opinion up as a fact.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can each statement be checked? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🔎 Fact","💭 Opinion"],"items":[["The Moon orbits the Earth.",0],["Chess is more fun than football.",1],["A spider has eight legs.",0],["Spiders are the scariest animals.",1],["London is the capital of the UK.",0],["Everyone should learn to swim.",1],["The bus was 10 minutes late.",0],["The bus driver was rude.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfvo","caption":"Flagship lab: swipe statements, find the judging word, split mixed sentences, tag advert tricks and highlight a news report.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_gZIo5oiBMt":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A book says: Frogs lay eggs in water. Tadpoles hatch from the eggs. What type of text is it?","choices":["A fact book","A poem","A story"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are these true things about real animals, or is something made up happening to a character?","explain":"It tells true facts about real frogs, so it is a fact book. It has no characters and no rhyme."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam wrote {a} poems. He drew the pictures for {b} of them, and Ann drew the rest. How many poems did Ann illustrate?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Ann drew the pictures for the poems Sam did not draw. Take {b} away from {a}.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}. Ann is the illustrator of {=a-b} poems, and Sam is the author of all {a}.","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A book starts with Once upon a time. What type of text is it most likely to be?","choices":["A story","A fact book","A recipe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which type of text starts by telling you a tale is about to begin?","explain":"Once upon a time is a clue that a story is starting, with characters and something that happens."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The cover says: by Lin Wu, pictures by Raj Dev. Who is the author?","choices":["Lin Wu","Raj Dev","Both of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"The word by comes before the author's name. The pictures name is the illustrator.","explain":"Lin Wu wrote the words, so Lin Wu is the author. Raj Dev drew the pictures, so Raj Dev is the illustrator."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A fact book about bugs has {n} labels on each page. How many labels are on 3 pages?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Labels name the parts in a picture. Add {n} three times.","explain":"{n} + {n} + {n} = {=3*n} labels on 3 pages.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Where can you find the names of the author and the illustrator?","choices":["On the front cover","Only on the last page","Inside the pictures"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the outside of the book, near the title.","explain":"Their names are on the front cover, near the title, and often on the first page inside too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A book has a shark on the cover. How can you find out if it is a story?","choices":["Look at the words inside","Guess from the shark","Count the pictures"],"answer":0,"hint":"A shark could be in a story or in a fact book. What tells you which?","explain":"Look inside: characters and once upon a time mean a story; true facts and labels mean a fact book."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Does every poem rhyme?","choices":["No, some poems do not rhyme","Yes, every poem rhymes","Only poems about animals rhyme"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rhyme is a clue for a poem, but think about poems with short lines and no rhyming words.","explain":"Many poems rhyme, but some do not. Short lines and playing with sounds are clues as well."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is from a poem?","choices":["Twinkle, twinkle, little star, how I wonder what you are","Owls hunt at night and sleep in the day","Once upon a time, an owl lost her way home"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for short lines and words that rhyme at the end.","explain":"Star and are rhyme, and it is said in a sing-song way. The owl facts are from a fact book, and once upon a time starts a story."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A poem has {=2*n} lines. Each two lines rhyme as a pair. How many rhyming pairs are there?","answer":"n","hint":"Put the lines into twos: lines 1 and 2, lines 3 and 4, and so on.","explain":"{=2*n} lines in twos make {n} rhyming pairs.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Who draws the pictures in a book?","choices":["The illustrator","The author","The library"],"answer":0,"hint":"One person writes the words and one person draws the pictures. Which name goes with pictures?","explain":"The illustrator draws the pictures. The author writes the words. Sometimes one person does both."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A shelf has {a} books. {b} of them are stories and the rest are fact books. How many fact books are there?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the stories away from all the books on the shelf.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}, so there are {=a-b} fact books.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["There are different types of text. A story tells about characters, and something happens to them. A poem has short lines. It often rhymes, like cat and hat. A fact book tells true things about the real world.","You cannot always tell from the cover. A book with a lion on it could be a story about a lion who sings. Or it could be a fact book about real lions. Look at the words inside for clues.","Clues for a story: Once upon a time, and The end. Clues for a poem: short lines and words that rhyme. Clues for a fact book: true facts, labels on photos, and a page that lists what is inside.","Two people can make a book. The author writes the words. The illustrator draws the pictures. Look for their names on the front cover. Sometimes one person does both jobs."],"adult":["This topic matches the US Common Core reading standards for Kindergarten and Grade 1. RL.K.5 asks children to recognise common types of texts, such as storybooks and poems. RL.K.6 and RI.K.6 ask them to name the author and illustrator and say what each one does. RL.1.5 asks them to explain the main differences between books that tell stories and books that give information. In England, Year 1 pupils meet key stories, rhymes, poems and non-fiction, and learn to talk about them.","Useful terms: fiction, non-fiction, story, poem, rhyme, title, front cover, author, illustrator, contents page, label and caption. A story has characters, a setting and events, often with a problem and an ending. A poem is set out in short lines and plays with sound, through rhyme, rhythm and repeated words. A fact book is organised to inform, with headings, labelled pictures and a contents page.","Common mistakes: judging the type from the cover picture; thinking any book with pictures is a story; thinking the author draws the pictures; thinking every poem must rhyme, when many do not; and saying fiction means lies. Fiction is made up on purpose to entertain, which is different from telling a lie. Be careful with picture books too, where one person may be both author and illustrator.","In the lab your child sorts six books onto Story, Poem and Facts shelves, where a lizard on a cover tempts a wrong guess. They tap the clue on three pages, drag a pencil and a paintbrush tag to the author and the illustrator on two covers, tap the rhyming lines in a short poem, and then write and draw their own little book of two different types."]},"familyTip":"At the library, pick three books together: a story, a book of poems and a fact book. Before you open each one, guess its type from the cover, then check the words inside and find the author and the illustrator.","keyIdea":"Stories, poems and fact books are different types of text. A story (fiction) has characters, and something happens to them. A poem has short lines and often rhymes. A fact book (non-fiction) gives true information about the real world. The cover can trick you, so look at the words inside for clues. The author writes the words and the illustrator draws the pictures; their names are on the front cover, and one person can do both jobs.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Story, poem or facts? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📖 Story","🎵 Poem","🔎 Facts"],"items":[["Once upon a time, a hen found some wheat.",0],["The little pig ran away, and the wolf ran after him.",0],["Jack and Jill went up the hill to fetch a pail of water.",1],["Wiggle, wiggle, little worm, twist and turn and squirm.",1],["Owls hunt at night and sleep in the day.",2],["The heart pumps blood all round the body.",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtexts","caption":"Flagship lab: sort six books onto shelves, tap the clue on each page, tag the author and the illustrator, find rhymes, then make your own book.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_tIi6L1n7kF":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"An actor stands stage left. Where is she, seen from the audience?","choices":["On the audience's right","On the audience's left","At the back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stage directions use the actor's view as they face the audience.","explain":"Stage left is the actor's left as they face the audience, so it is on the audience's right."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A director wants a ghost scene to feel eerie. Which lighting fits best?","choices":["Cold, dim blue light","Warm, bright light on the whole stage","Normal house lights"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which light hides detail and feels unsettling?","explain":"Cold, dim blue light feels eerie and hides detail. Warm, bright light feels safe and open."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is blocking?","choices":["The planned movement and position of actors on stage","Stopping an actor from speaking","Building the set"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is about where actors stand and move.","explain":"Blocking is planned with the director: where each actor stands and moves, and when."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In one production of Macbeth, the audience sees a glowing dagger. In another, the air is empty. What does the empty air suggest?","choices":["The dagger is in his mind, a sign of guilt","The dagger is really there","The director forgot the prop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Macbeth himself asks if it is \"a dagger of the mind\".","explain":"If only Macbeth sees it, the production suggests his guilty mind creates it, which the words \"a dagger of the mind\" support."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Two productions of a play each make {c} choices about light, set and acting. If they make every choice differently, how many different choices are there altogether?","answer":"2*c","hint":"Each production has its own {c} choices. Add them.","explain":"{c} + {c} = {=2*c} different choices. The script is the same, but each set of choices gives a new interpretation.","params":{"c":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A director stages a play in a bare space with one chair. What kind of set is this?","choices":["Minimalist","Naturalistic","Expressionist"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it copy a real room, distort it, or use very little?","explain":"A minimalist set uses very little, which keeps the focus on the actors and allows quick scene changes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A production sets Romeo and Juliet in a modern city but keeps every word. What can you say about it?","choices":["It departs from the setting but keeps the script's words","It is not Romeo and Juliet any more","It is exactly the same as the original"],"answer":0,"hint":"Separate what changed (where and when) from what stayed (the text).","explain":"The setting departs from Shakespeare's Verona, but the words are faithful. You can then judge whether the new setting helps the play's meaning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The lighting plan has {a} cues in Act 1 and {b} cues in Act 2. How many lighting cues does the operator run in the whole show?","answer":"a+b","hint":"A cue is one planned change of light. Add the two acts.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} cues. Each cue is a choice about mood or focus, so lighting is planned as carefully as the acting.","params":{"a":[8,20],"b":[6,18]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An actor is told to move downstage. Where does she go?","choices":["Towards the audience","Towards the back of the stage","Under the stage"],"answer":0,"hint":"Old stages sloped down towards the seats.","explain":"Downstage is the part of the stage nearest the audience. Upstage is the back."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A play has {s} scenes, each in a different place. A naturalistic production builds a full, detailed set for each one. How many set changes are needed between scenes?","answer":"s-1","hint":"The first set is already in place when the play starts. Count the changes after that.","explain":"The first scene starts on its set, so {s} scenes need {=s-1} changes. That is why many productions choose a minimalist set instead.","params":{"s":[4,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The line is \"I'm so happy for you.\" The actor says it through gritted teeth and walks away. What is the subtext?","choices":["She is not happy for them","She is very happy for them","There is no subtext"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do her voice and movement agree with her words?","explain":"Gritted teeth and walking away contradict the words, so the audience reads that she is not really happy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A scene has {n} lines in the script. The director cuts {c} of them to speed it up. How many lines are still spoken?","answer":"n-c","hint":"Start with all the lines in the script and take away the cuts.","explain":"{n} - {c} = {=n-c} lines are spoken. Cutting lines is one way a production can depart from the script, so ask whether the cuts change the meaning.","params":{"n":[40,80],"c":[5,15]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A script gives the words. It does not tell you everything. Sarah says, \"I'm fine.\" If she smiles and hugs Tom, she means it. If she pauses, looks down and turns away, she is hurt. The words stay the same. The meaning changes.","That hidden meaning is called subtext. Actors show it with their voice, their pauses and their body. Directors and designers add more: light, sound, the set and where people stand.","Stage directions use the actor's view. Stage left is the actor's left as they face the audience, so from your seat it is on the right. Downstage is the front, nearest you. Upstage is the back.","Two productions can stage one scene in very different ways. In one Macbeth, we see a glowing dagger. In another, the air is empty and his hand shakes. Both use the same words, but they suggest different things."],"adult":["This topic is about plays as performance, not just text. The English national curriculum for Key Stage 3 asks pupils to see how a play is brought to life on stage. It also asks how different staging can give different readings of the same play. In the US Common Core, grades 6 to 8 compare a script with a staged or filmed version. They judge how far it stays true to the script (RL.6.7 to RL.8.7).","Key terms. Subtext is the meaning under the words. Stage directions are the playwright's written notes. Blocking is where actors stand and move. Proxemics is the space between characters: far apart can show conflict. A naturalistic set copies a real place. A minimalist set uses very little. An expressionist set bends shapes and colours to show a character's mind.","Common mistakes. Pupils often treat the script as the whole play and only analyse the words. Many mix up stage left and stage right. These are given from the actor's view, facing the audience. Some think downstage means under the stage, but it is the front. Be careful, too, with calling a production wrong just because it is different. Ask instead: do the words support this choice?","In the lab your child directs one line to show two meanings and lights three scenes. They follow stage directions on a grid seen from the seats, match sets to a director's aim, and sort four stagings of Macbeth's dagger."]},"familyTip":"Watch two different clips of the same scene from a play online (Shakespeare scenes are easy to find). Ask: what did each director or actor choose differently, and which version did you believe more? Why?","keyIdea":"A play script is only part of a play. The director, designers and actors make choices that give the words their meaning on stage: an actor's tone, pauses and body language show the subtext, lighting sets the mood and tells the audience where to look, blocking places actors on stage, and the set tells the audience what kind of world they are in. Because each production makes different choices, two productions of the same script can interpret it differently, and both can still be faithful to the text if the words support them.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Who makes this choice? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✍️ The playwright, in the script","🎬 The production, on stage"],"items":[["The words the characters say",0],["A cold blue light for the ghost scene",1],["Exit, pursued by a bear (a stage direction)",0],["The actor pauses before answering",1],["The order of the scenes",0],["Setting the play in a modern office",1],["A list of the characters",0],["The two lovers stand far apart",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstage","caption":"Flagship lab: direct one line two ways, light three scenes, follow stage directions, choose a set and sort four stagings of Macbeth's dagger.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ujwtRoYJ34":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a group of lines in a poem called?","choices":["A stanza","A chapter","A scene"],"answer":0,"hint":"Chapters are for novels and scenes are for plays.","explain":"A group of lines in a poem, set off by a gap, is a stanza."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A page has character names with colons and words in brackets like (She opens the door.). What kind of text is it?","choices":["A play script","A poem","A novel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which kind of text has stage directions?","explain":"Character names and stage directions in brackets show a play script, which is split into acts and scenes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A play has {a} acts, and each act has 3 scenes. How many scenes are in the play?","answer":"3*a","hint":"Each act holds 3 scenes. Multiply.","explain":"{a} acts with 3 scenes each make {=3*a} scenes.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Put these in order from biggest to smallest: scene, play, act.","choices":["Play, act, scene","Scene, act, play","Act, play, scene"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part holds all the others?","explain":"The play holds the acts, and each act holds scenes: play, act, scene."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Stanza 1 of a poem is about a calm sea. Stanza 2 is about a storm. How does stanza 2 build on stanza 1?","choices":["It changes the mood from calm to wild","It repeats stanza 1 word for word","It has nothing to do with stanza 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the feeling of the two stanzas.","explain":"The same sea goes from calm to stormy, so stanza 2 builds on stanza 1 by changing its mood."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A poem has {s} stanzas with 5 lines in each. How many stanzas does it have?","answer":"s","hint":"The question asks for stanzas, not lines.","explain":"It has {s} stanzas. The {=5*s} lines are the total number of lines, not stanzas.","params":{"s":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word in a new chapter shows that it builds on an earlier one?","choices":["\"Still holding the key, Ama...\"","\"Once upon a time...\"","\"Chapter 1\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a word that points back to something that already happened.","explain":"\"Still\" points back: the key was found in an earlier chapter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a play, the action moves from a kitchen to a garden the next day. What usually starts?","choices":["A new scene","A new stanza","A new chapter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of a play often changes with the place or time?","explain":"A new place or time in a play usually means a new scene."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A play script says \"ACT 2, SCENE 1\". Which part is bigger?","choices":["Act 2","Scene 1","They are the same size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part holds the other one inside it?","explain":"An act is a big part of a play that holds several scenes, so Act 2 is bigger."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A poem has {s} stanzas, and each stanza has 4 lines. How many lines does the poem have?","answer":"4*s","hint":"Each stanza is a group of 4 lines. Multiply by the number of stanzas.","explain":"{s} stanzas of 4 lines make {=4*s} lines. There are still only {s} stanzas.","params":{"s":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Chapter 4 begins: \"Still wet from the river, Sam ran home.\" What does this tell you?","choices":["An earlier chapter showed Sam in the river","This is the first chapter","The chapters are not linked"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word points back to something that already happened?","explain":"\"Still wet\" points back. An earlier chapter must show how Sam got wet, so Chapter 4 builds on it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A poem has {=3*k} lines split into stanzas of 3 lines each. How many stanzas does it have?","answer":"k","hint":"Each stanza is a group of 3 lines. How many groups of 3 fit in {=3*k}?","explain":"{=3*k} lines in groups of 3 make {k} stanzas.","params":{"k":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A novel is a long story. It is split into chapters. Each chapter has a number, and often a title, like Chapter 3: The Gate.","A play is split into acts, and each act is split into scenes. A new scene often means a new place or time. In the script you see the names of the characters and stage directions in brackets.","A poem is split into stanzas. A stanza is a group of lines with a gap after it. If a poem has 12 lines in 3 groups, it has 3 stanzas, not 12.","Each part builds on the parts before. In Chapter 3, Ama is still holding the key she found in Chapter 1. In a poem, the second stanza can change the mood of the first. Say which part you mean and how it links back."],"adult":["This matches Common Core RL.3.5: refer to parts of stories, dramas and poems using terms such as chapter, scene and stanza, and describe how each part builds on earlier sections. In England it sits in the Year 3 and 4 reading programme. Pupils learn to recognise some forms of poetry, to tell prose, play scripts and poetry apart, and to see how structure helps meaning.","Key terms: prose, novel, chapter and paragraph; play script, act, scene, cast list, dialogue and stage directions; poem, stanza, line and verse. A stanza is a group of lines set off by a blank line. Poets sometimes say verse for a stanza, but verse can also mean poetry in general. The parts nest: a play holds acts, an act holds scenes, and a scene holds lines of dialogue.","Common mistakes: counting lines when asked for stanzas; calling every part a chapter, even in a poem or play; thinking a scene is bigger than an act; and treating each chapter or stanza as a stand-alone piece. Ask your child to find the words that point back, such as still, again or once more.","In the lab, learners name a part from how the page looks, number the stanzas of two poems before they count them, and order the parts of a play, a novel and a poem by size. They put four mixed-up chapters in order using clues that point back, and say how each stanza of a short sea poem builds on the one before."]},"familyTip":"When you read a book or poem together, ask: which chapter or stanza is this, and what does it add to the one before? On a play or film night, spot when a new scene starts: a new place or a new time.","keyIdea":"Each kind of text has its own words for its parts. A novel is split into chapters, a play into acts and scenes, and a poem into stanzas, which are groups of lines. Big parts hold smaller parts: an act holds scenes, a chapter holds paragraphs. Each new part builds on the ones before, so when you talk about a text, name the part and say what it adds to what came earlier.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which kind of text has this part? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📖 Novel","🎭 Play","📝 Poem"],"items":[["Chapter",0],["Scene",1],["Stanza",2],["Act",1],["Paragraph",0],["Stage directions",1],["Groups of lines with gaps",2],["Numbered chapter titles",0],["Rhyming lines",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagparts","caption":"Flagship lab: name text parts, count stanzas, order parts by size, put chapters in order and see how stanzas build.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
